# Copy to `.env` and fill in. Nothing here is secret in development.

# ── Database ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# One local Postgres needs only DATABASE_URL. Supabase needs both — see below.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres@localhost:5432/flatirons?schema=public"

# Only for Supabase (or any pooled Postgres). Migrations and the seed use this;
# the app uses DATABASE_URL. Unset, both fall back to DATABASE_URL, which is
# correct for a local server and for CI.
#
# DIRECT_URL="postgresql://postgres.<ref>:<password>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres"

# ── Supabase, in full ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Project settings → Database → Connection string. Take BOTH from the
# "Connection pooling" section, and mind the port — the hostname is identical
# and only the port distinguishes the two modes:
#
#   DATABASE_URL  ...pooler.supabase.com:6543   transaction mode, for the app
#   DIRECT_URL    ...pooler.supabase.com:5432   session mode, for migrations
#
# Do NOT use the `db.<ref>.supabase.co:5432` string the dashboard shows first.
# That host is IPv6-only, so it fails on GitHub Actions and on any IPv4-only
# machine with ENETUNREACH — a network error that reads like an outage rather
# than a wrong hostname. The pooler host above is reachable over IPv4 and
# gives migrations the same session-mode connection.
#
# Why two at all: the migration engine takes an advisory lock and runs
# multi-statement DDL that has to stay on one server connection, and a
# transaction pooler hands out a different backend per transaction. Point
# `db:deploy` at 6543 and it hangs on the lock or half-applies.
#
# Supabase appends `?sslmode=require`. Keep it. Note that node-postgres 8.23
# treats `require` as full certificate verification, which is what you want
# and what the pooler's publicly-signed certificate satisfies.

# Optional. Connections per pool; defaults to 5. Raise it only for a single
# long-lived server (`next start` on one VM). Leave it alone on serverless,
# where every warm instance holds its own pool and they add up.
# DATABASE_POOL_MAX="5"

# ── Site ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Read by src/lib/site-url.ts — the sitemap, and the return address on
# magic-link emails and OAuth redirects. Falls back to the production
# hostname, so auth on a deployed environment NEEDS this set correctly.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL="http://localhost:3000"

# ── Auth (Supabase) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Both unset: auth is OFF and the portal is open to anyone with a reference —
# fine for local hacking and CI, not for production. Both set: /move/[id]
# requires a session, customers sign in by magic link, staff by GitHub.
#
# Project settings → API. The publishable ("anon"/sb_publishable_…) key is
# designed to be public — it ships in the page. The secret key is NOT used by
# this app; never put it in any environment.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL="https://<ref>.supabase.co"
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY="sb_publishable_…"

# Staff, comma-separated, case-insensitive. A signed-in email on this list
# may open any move. Unset means no staff — never everyone.
# STAFF_EMAILS="you@example.com"

# ── Payments (Stripe) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Unset: "Pay now" is the prototype's bookkeeping flip — no money moves.
# Set: the button redirects to Stripe Checkout, and the signed webhook is the
# only thing that marks a job paid.
#
# Developers → API keys. Secret key only — the app renders no Stripe UI, so
# no publishable key is needed. NEVER paste this key into a chat or commit
# it; on Vercel it belongs in the Production environment variables.
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY="sk_…"
#
# Developers → Webhooks → Add endpoint:
#   https://<your-domain>/api/stripe/webhook   events: checkout.session.completed
# The signing secret it issues:
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET="whsec_…"
